//? Title: Alpha-Beta Pruning: Minimax Without the Wasted Work
//? Subtitle: Why the window is exact, the Knuth-Moore minimal tree, Pearl's branching factor, and the complete game of tic-tac-toe with datamunge's algorithms::alpha_beta
//? Exports: both
//? Lang: en-GB

\abstract(
Minimax values a game position by searching every line of play, so its cost
grows as \(b^d\) for branching factor \(b\) and depth \(d\). Alpha-beta
pruning returns the same value while skipping lines that cannot change it.
We prove why the pruning is safe, derive the Knuth-Moore count of the leaves
it must still examine with perfect move ordering,
\(b^{\lceil d/2\rceil}+b^{\lfloor d/2\rfloor}-1\), and state Pearl's
branching factor for random ordering. The library's `order_moves` reproduces
the Knuth-Moore count exactly. On random trees, measured effective branching
factors approach Pearl's values (1.618, 2.148 and 2.630 for \(b=2,3,4\)) from
above. The data is the complete game tree of tic-tac-toe: 255 168 games and
549 946 positions. Minimax confirms that the game is a draw. Alpha-beta
reaches the same verdict from 7 330 of the 255 168 final positions in the
natural move order, 781 with perfect ordering, and 84 100 with the worst.
)

\toc

> The data: every game of tic-tac-toe

`algorithms::tictactoe::game_tree()` builds the full game tree from the
empty board. X moves first and is the maximising player. Every finished
game is a leaf worth \(+1\) if X won, \(-1\) if O won and \(0\) for a draw.
Squares are numbered 0 to 8 in reading order. The table splits the tree by
X's opening square. Each count of games is the number of leaves, so it is
also the number of final positions plain minimax evaluates.

```{cpp, results="markdown", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>

namespace alg = dm::algorithms;

int main() {
  const auto tree = alg::tictactoe::game_tree();
  const auto mm = alg::minimax(tree, true);
  const auto ab = alg::alpha_beta(tree, true);
  std::print("{} games, {} positions; minimax value {} (alpha-beta: {}).\n\n", mm.leaves_evaluated,
             mm.nodes_visited, mm.value, ab.value);

  std::print("| opening square | kind | games | X wins | O wins | draws | value |\n"
             "| ---: | :--- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n");
  for (int square = 0; square < 9; ++square) {
    const auto& child = tree.children[square];
    long long wins[3] = {0, 0, 0};  // O win, draw, X win
    auto count = [&](auto&& self, const alg::GameNode& n) -> void {
      if (n.leaf) { ++wins[static_cast<int>(n.value) + 1]; return; }
      for (const auto& c : n.children) self(self, c);
    };
    count(count, child);
    const auto r = alg::minimax(child, false);
    const char* kind = square == 4 ? "centre" : (square % 2 == 0 ? "corner" : "edge");
    std::print("| {} | {} | {} | {} | {} | {} | {} |\n", square, kind, r.leaves_evaluated, wins[2],
               wins[0], wins[1], r.value);
  }
}
```
// result_begin: markdown
255168 games, 549946 positions; minimax value 0 (alpha-beta: 0).

| opening square | kind | games | X wins | O wins | draws | value |
| ---: | :--- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |
| 0 | corner | 27732 | 14652 | 7896 | 5184 | 0 |
| 1 | edge | 29592 | 14232 | 10176 | 5184 | 0 |
| 2 | corner | 27732 | 14652 | 7896 | 5184 | 0 |
| 3 | edge | 29592 | 14232 | 10176 | 5184 | 0 |
| 4 | centre | 25872 | 15648 | 5616 | 4608 | 0 |
| 5 | edge | 29592 | 14232 | 10176 | 5184 | 0 |
| 6 | corner | 27732 | 14652 | 7896 | 5184 | 0 |
| 7 | edge | 29592 | 14232 | 10176 | 5184 | 0 |
| 8 | corner | 27732 | 14652 | 7896 | 5184 | 0 |
// result_end

The totals are the classical counts: 255 168 games, of which 131 184 are
X wins, 77 904 O wins and 46 080 draws. Opening in a corner leads to
27 732 possible games, on an edge to 29 592 and in the centre to 25 872.
Every opening has value 0, so with best play from both sides tic-tac-toe is
a draw. X wins more of the *possible* games simply because X moves first
and gets an extra mark. Minimax had to evaluate all 255 168 leaves to
establish this.

> Minimax and the alpha-beta window

>> Minimax

Write \(V(n)\) for the value of node \(n\) under best play. A leaf has its
own score. A max node takes the largest child value and a min node the
smallest:

$$
V(n)=\begin{cases}\text{score}(n) & n \text{ a leaf},\\ \max_{c}V(c) & \text{max to move},\\ \min_c V(c) & \text{min to move}.\end{cases}
$$
\alttext(V of n is the score of n at a leaf, the maximum of V of c over the children c when max is to move, and the minimum of V of c when min is to move.)

Computing \(V\) this way touches every node.

>> The window and why it is safe

Alpha-beta evaluates a node with a *window* \((\alpha,\beta)\). Let
\(F(n,\alpha,\beta)\) be the value the routine returns. The claim, by
induction on height, is

$$
F\le\alpha \Rightarrow V\le F,\qquad
\alpha<F<\beta \Rightarrow F=V,\qquad
F\ge\beta \Rightarrow V\ge F .
$$
\alttext(If F is at most alpha then V is at most F; if F is strictly between alpha and beta then F equals V; if F is at least beta then V is at least F.)

So inside the window the answer is exact, and outside it is a bound in the
right direction. At a max node the routine raises \(\alpha\) to the best
child value found so far and stops as soon as \(\alpha\ge\beta\). Stopping
is safe: the node's value is already at least \(\beta\), which by the
claim is all the caller needs. A min node one level up compares this value
with its own \(\beta\), which came from a sibling line it already has, so
it will never choose this node. The min node is symmetric. Started with
\((-\infty,+\infty)\), the window never excludes the root value, so
alpha-beta returns exactly \(V(\text{root})\).

```
ALPHA-BETA(n, maximizing, alpha, beta):
    if n is a leaf: return score(n)
    if maximizing:
        best = -inf
        for each child c:
            best  = max(best, ALPHA-BETA(c, false, alpha, beta))
            alpha = max(alpha, best)
            if alpha >= beta: break          # cutoff
        return best
    else: the same with min, beta updated, and the same test
```

> How many leaves survive?

>> Perfect ordering: the Knuth-Moore minimal tree

Take a uniform tree with distinct leaf values, where every node's best child
is searched first. Knuth and Moore (1975) sort the nodes that alpha-beta
visits into three types:

- *Type 1*: the root, and the first child of every type-1 node. These lie on
  the principal variation and are searched with a full window. All their
  children are visited.
- *Type 2*: every other child of a type-1 node, and the first child of each
  type-3 node. The first child of such a node already refutes the move
  (it causes a cutoff), so only one child is visited.
- *Type 3*: the visited child of a type-2 node. Its parent's bound cannot be
  beaten by any of its children, so all of them are visited.

Count the leaves by asking what each player has to *prove*. Let \(v\) be
the root value. To show \(V\ge v\), max needs a *strategy*: one good move
at each max node, but an answer to every move at each min node. The leaves
of that strategy form a subtree that branches \(b\) ways at min levels
only. The root is a max node, so min moves at depths \(1,3,5,\dots\) below
\(d\), and there are \(\lfloor d/2\rfloor\) such levels. The proof has
\(b^{\lfloor d/2\rfloor}\) leaves. To show \(V\le v\), min needs a strategy
that branches at the \(\lceil d/2\rceil\) max levels, giving
\(b^{\lceil d/2\rceil}\) leaves. With distinct leaf values the two proofs
share exactly one leaf, the end of the principal variation. The type-1 and
type-3 nodes above are exactly where these strategies branch. Hence

$$
L_{\min}(b,d)=b^{\lceil d/2\rceil}+b^{\lfloor d/2\rfloor}-1 .
$$
\alttext(The minimal number of leaves is b to the ceiling of d over 2, plus b to the floor of d over 2, minus 1.)

Any correct algorithm must examine both proofs, so \(L_{\min}\) is also a
lower bound for every ordering. Its growth rate is \(\sqrt b\) per ply, so
perfect ordering lets alpha-beta search twice as deep for the same cost.

>> Random ordering: Pearl's branching factor

With children in random order and leaf values independent and continuous,
Pearl (1982) showed that alpha-beta evaluates about \(R_b^{\,d}\) leaves for
large \(d\), where

$$
R_b=\frac{\xi_b}{1-\xi_b},\qquad \xi_b^{\,b}+\xi_b-1=0,\ \ 0<\xi_b<1 .
$$
\alttext(R sub b equals xi sub b over 1 minus xi sub b, where xi sub b is the root between 0 and 1 of xi to the b plus xi minus 1 equals 0.)

\(\xi_b\) is the fixed point of the probability that a uniform tree's
minimax value lies below a given threshold, as the depth grows. For \(b=2\)
the equation is \(\xi^2+\xi-1=0\), so \(\xi_2=(\sqrt5-1)/2\) and
\(R_2=(1+\sqrt5)/2\), the golden ratio. For large \(b\),
\(R_b\approx b^{3/4}\). Random order therefore sits between the minimal
tree's \(\sqrt b\) and minimax's \(b\). `alpha_beta_branching_factor` solves
the equation by bisection.

>> Checking both results on uniform trees

`random_game_tree(d, b, seed)` builds a uniform tree with independent
Uniform(0, 1) leaves. `order_moves` sorts every node's children by their
minimax values: best first for the minimal tree, worst first for the
opposite. The first figure uses \(b=3\) and averages \(\log_{10}\) leaf
counts over 30 trees per depth.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/alpha_beta_ordering.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>

namespace alg = dm::algorithms;

int main() {
  std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
  const int b = 3, trials = 30;
  std::vector<double> depth, all, worst, given, best, km;
  bool km_exact = true;
  std::print("| depth | minimax b^d | worst first | random order | best first | Knuth-Moore |\n"
             "| ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n");
  for (int d = 1; d <= 10; ++d) {
    double lw = 0, lg = 0, lb = 0;
    for (int t = 0; t < trials; ++t) {
      const auto tree = alg::random_game_tree(d, b, 100 * d + t);
      lw += std::log10(alg::alpha_beta(alg::order_moves(tree, true, alg::MoveOrder::WorstFirst), true).leaves_evaluated);
      lg += std::log10(alg::alpha_beta(tree, true).leaves_evaluated);
      const auto bf = alg::alpha_beta(alg::order_moves(tree, true, alg::MoveOrder::BestFirst), true);
      lb += std::log10(bf.leaves_evaluated);
      km_exact = km_exact && bf.leaves_evaluated == alg::minimal_tree_leaves(b, d);
    }
    depth.push_back(d);
    all.push_back(d * std::log10(b));
    worst.push_back(lw / trials);
    given.push_back(lg / trials);
    best.push_back(lb / trials);
    km.push_back(std::log10(alg::minimal_tree_leaves(b, d)));
    if (d % 2 == 0)
      std::print("| {} | {} | {:.0f} | {:.0f} | {:.0f} | {} |\n", d, static_cast<long long>(std::pow(b, d)),
                 std::pow(10, worst.back()), std::pow(10, given.back()), std::pow(10, best.back()),
                 alg::minimal_tree_leaves(b, d));
  }
  std::print("\nBest-first count equals the Knuth-Moore formula in every one of the {} trees: {}.\n"
             "Leaf counts are geometric means over {} trees.\n", 10 * trials, km_exact ? "yes" : "no", trials);

  dm::graphics::RPlot::create()
      .lines(depth, all, "minimax", dm::RGB{17, 24, 39}, 2.0)
      .lines(depth, worst, "worst first", dm::RGB{220, 38, 38}, 2.0)
      .lines(depth, given, "random", dm::RGB{37, 99, 235}, 2.0)
      .lines(depth, km, "Knuth-Moore", dm::RGB{245, 158, 11}, 5.0)
      .points(depth, best, "best first", dm::RGB{5, 150, 105}, 5.0)
      .y_limits(0, 6.5)
      .title("Leaves evaluated, b = 3")
      .x_label("depth d")
      .y_label("log10 leaves")
      .size(860, 500)
      .save_svg("build/alpha_beta_ordering.svg");
}
```
// result_begin: markdown
| depth | minimax b^d | worst first | random order | best first | Knuth-Moore |
| ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |
| 2 | 9 | 9 | 7 | 5 | 5 |
| 4 | 81 | 79 | 45 | 17 | 17 |
| 6 | 729 | 649 | 240 | 53 | 53 |
| 8 | 6561 | 5168 | 1305 | 161 | 161 |
| 10 | 59049 | 39680 | 6192 | 485 | 485 |

Best-first count equals the Knuth-Moore formula in every one of the 300 trees: yes.
Leaf counts are geometric means over 30 trees.
\image(figures/alpha_beta_ordering.svg)
// result_end

\caption(Leaves evaluated against depth on a log scale, for uniform trees with \(b=3\). The green best-first points sit on the thick amber Knuth-Moore line. Random order (blue) grows more slowly than worst-first order (red), which in turn is well below minimax (black).)
\alttext(Line plot of log10 leaves against depth from 1 to 10. Minimax is a straight line rising to log10 of 59049, about 4.8. Worst-first ordering rises almost as steeply, to about 4.6. Random ordering rises to about 3.8. Best-first points lie exactly on the Knuth-Moore line, which rises in a staircase to about 2.7.)

Best-first ordering matched the Knuth-Moore count exactly in all 300
trees. Ordering is everything here. At depth 10 the best order needs 485
leaves, random order about 6 200 and the worst order about 40 000, out of
59 049. Even the worst order prunes something, because bounds
inherited from further up the tree still cut off deep lines.

Pearl's formula is an asymptote, so the next block measures the local rate.
For each depth it takes \(\sqrt{L(d)/L(d-2)}\), the growth per ply over two
plies, which cancels the odd/even staircase. \(L\) is the geometric mean
leaf count over 40 random-order trees.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/alpha_beta_pearl.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>

namespace alg = dm::algorithms;

int main() {
  std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
  const int trials = 40;
  auto plot = dm::graphics::RPlot::create();
  std::print("| b | deepest d | local rate at d | Pearl R_b | b^(3/4) | sqrt(b) |\n"
             "| ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n");
  const std::vector<std::tuple<int, int, dm::RGB>> runs = {
      {2, 18, dm::RGB{37, 99, 235}}, {3, 12, dm::RGB{220, 38, 38}}, {4, 9, dm::RGB{5, 150, 105}}};
  for (const auto& [b, dmax, colour] : runs) {
    std::vector<double> log_leaves(dmax + 1, 0.0), d_axis, rate, pearl;
    for (int d = 1; d <= dmax; ++d)
      for (int t = 0; t < trials; ++t)
        log_leaves[d] += std::log(alg::alpha_beta(alg::random_game_tree(d, b, 7919 * d + t), true)
                                      .leaves_evaluated) / trials;
    const double R = alg::alpha_beta_branching_factor(b);
    for (int d = 3; d <= dmax; ++d) {
      d_axis.push_back(d);
      rate.push_back(std::exp((log_leaves[d] - log_leaves[d - 2]) / 2));
      pearl.push_back(R);
    }
    plot.lines(d_axis, rate, std::format("b = {}", b), colour, 2.0)
        .lines(d_axis, pearl, "", colour, 1.0);
    std::print("| {} | {} | {:.3f} | {:.3f} | {:.3f} | {:.3f} |\n", b, dmax, rate.back(), R,
               std::pow(b, 0.75), std::sqrt(b));
  }
  plot.title("Local branching factor of alpha-beta, random order")
      .x_label("depth d")
      .y_label("sqrt(L(d) / L(d-2))")
      .size(860, 500)
      .save_svg("build/alpha_beta_pearl.svg");
}
```
// result_begin: markdown
| b | deepest d | local rate at d | Pearl R_b | b^(3/4) | sqrt(b) |
| ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |
| 2 | 18 | 1.686 | 1.618 | 1.682 | 1.414 |
| 3 | 12 | 2.292 | 2.148 | 2.280 | 1.732 |
| 4 | 9 | 2.712 | 2.630 | 2.828 | 2.000 |
\image(figures/alpha_beta_pearl.svg)
// result_end

\caption(The local branching factor for \(b=2,3,4\) (thick lines) and Pearl's asymptotic value \(R_b\) (thin lines of the same colour). Each curve starts well above its asymptote and drifts down towards it as the trees deepen.)
\alttext(Three thick noisy curves falling with depth: blue for b equal 2 from about 1.8 at depth 3 to about 1.65 to 1.7 beyond depth 13, above a thin line at 1.618; red for b equal 3 from about 2.5 to about 2.25 to 2.3, above a thin line at 2.148; green for b equal 4 from about 3.15 to about 2.7, above a thin line at 2.63.)

Every measured rate is above Pearl's value and falling towards it. The
approach is slow: at the deepest trees searched the gap is 0.07 for
\(b=2\), 0.14 for \(b=3\) and 0.08 for \(b=4\). This is expected: Pearl's
argument is a limit, and the probability \(\xi_b\) only settles once the
tree is deep enough for the root value's distribution to sharpen. The
finite-depth rates happen to sit close to \(b^{3/4}\), but that is a
coincidence of the depths reachable here. Random order is clearly far from
the \(\sqrt b\) of the minimal tree. Engines therefore invest heavily in
move ordering (killer moves, history tables, iterative deepening), to move
from \(R_b\) towards \(\sqrt b\).

> Back to tic-tac-toe: ordering and windows

Tic-tac-toe is not a uniform tree: the branching factor falls from 9 to 1,
games end early, and the leaf values are only \(-1\), \(0\) and \(+1\). The
last point matters. Ties mean that \(\alpha\ge\beta\) is often reached with
equality, which the uniform-tree analysis excludes. The next block compares
orderings on the full tree. It also tries narrow windows, which ask a yes/no
question instead of computing a value. With the window \((-0.5,0.5)\),
alpha-beta returns a value strictly inside it only if the game is a draw.
With \((0.5,1.5)\) it asks whether X can force a win, and with
\((-1.5,-0.5)\) whether O can.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/alpha_beta_tictactoe.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>

namespace alg = dm::algorithms;

int main() {
  std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
  const auto tree = alg::tictactoe::game_tree();
  struct Row { std::string search; alg::GameResult r; };
  const auto inf = std::numeric_limits<double>::infinity();
  const std::vector<Row> rows = {
      {"minimax", alg::minimax(tree, true)},
      {"alpha-beta, worst first", alg::alpha_beta(alg::order_moves(tree, true, alg::MoveOrder::WorstFirst), true)},
      {"alpha-beta, square order", alg::alpha_beta(tree, true, -inf, inf)},
      {"alpha-beta, best first", alg::alpha_beta(alg::order_moves(tree, true, alg::MoveOrder::BestFirst), true)},
      {"window (0.5, 1.5): can X force a win?", alg::alpha_beta(tree, true, 0.5, 1.5)},
      {"window (-0.5, 0.5): is it a draw?", alg::alpha_beta(tree, true, -0.5, 0.5)},
      {"window (-1.5, -0.5): can O force a win?", alg::alpha_beta(tree, true, -1.5, -0.5)},
  };
  std::print("| search | returned | leaves | positions | cutoffs | share of leaves |\n"
             "| :--- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n");
  std::vector<double> x, y;
  std::vector<std::string> labels;
  for (std::size_t i = 0; i < rows.size(); ++i) {
    const auto& [name, r] = rows[i];
    std::print("| {} | {} | {} | {} | {} | {:.2f}% |\n", name, r.value, r.leaves_evaluated, r.nodes_visited,
               r.cutoffs, 100.0 * r.leaves_evaluated / 255168);
    x.push_back(i + 1.0);
    y.push_back(std::log10(r.leaves_evaluated));
  }
  dm::graphics::RPlot::create()
      .bars(x, y, "", dm::RGB{37, 99, 235})
      .x_ticks(x, {"minimax", "worst", "squares", "best", "X wins?", "draw?", "O wins?"})
      .x_label("")
      .title("Final positions evaluated, tic-tac-toe")
      .y_label("log10 leaves")
      .size(860, 480)
      .save_svg("build/alpha_beta_tictactoe.svg");
}
```
// result_begin: markdown
| search | returned | leaves | positions | cutoffs | share of leaves |
| :--- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |
| minimax | 0 | 255168 | 549946 | 0 | 100.00% |
| alpha-beta, worst first | 0 | 84100 | 198837 | 90393 | 32.96% |
| alpha-beta, square order | 0 | 7330 | 18297 | 8180 | 2.87% |
| alpha-beta, best first | 0 | 781 | 2312 | 1060 | 0.31% |
| window (0.5, 1.5): can X force a win? | 0 | 6988 | 17479 | 7839 | 2.74% |
| window (-0.5, 0.5): is it a draw? | 0 | 6740 | 16811 | 7795 | 2.64% |
| window (-1.5, -0.5): can O force a win? | 0 | 462 | 1132 | 520 | 0.18% |
\image(figures/alpha_beta_tictactoe.svg)
// result_end

\caption(Leaves evaluated on the full tic-tac-toe tree on a log scale, for minimax, three move orders of alpha-beta and three narrow-window tests.)
\alttext(Bar chart of log10 leaves. Minimax is tallest at about 5.4. Worst-first alpha-beta is about 4.9, square-order alpha-beta about 3.9, best-first about 2.9. The X-wins window test is about 3.8, the draw window test about 3.8, and the O-wins window test is lowest at about 2.7.)

Every search agrees on the value 0. Square order, which tries squares in
reading order, already avoids 97% of the final positions. The coarse
values help: a tie with the bound already counts as a cutoff
(\(\alpha\ge\beta\)), and with only three possible values ties are common.
Perfect ordering needs only 781 leaves, 0.31% of the game. The worst order
still prunes two thirds, because bounds inherited from above cut off deep
lines, as on the uniform trees.

The windows read as the bound rule says. For "can X force a win?" the
result 0 is at most \(\alpha=0.5\), a fail low, so \(V\le0\) and the answer
is no. For "can O force a win?" the result 0 is at least \(\beta=-0.5\), a
fail high, so \(V\ge0\) and again the answer is no. That test is the
cheapest of all, 462 leaves: X only has to show one line that avoids
losing. The draw window returns 0 strictly inside \((-0.5,0.5)\), an exact
answer, from 6 740 leaves, fewer than the 7 330 of the full window. Narrow
windows prune more because \(\alpha\ge\beta\) is reached sooner, which is the
idea behind aspiration search, PVS/NegaScout and MTD(f).

> Summary

- Alpha-beta returns the exact minimax value. Inside the window the answer
  is exact; outside it is a bound in the right direction, so cutoffs are
  safe.
- With perfect ordering it examines
  \(b^{\lceil d/2\rceil}+b^{\lfloor d/2\rfloor}-1\) leaves, about
  \(\sqrt b\) per ply, and no algorithm can do with fewer.
  `order_moves(…, MoveOrder::BestFirst)` reproduces the count exactly.
- With random ordering on continuous leaves the rate is Pearl's
  \(R_b=\xi_b/(1-\xi_b)\), close to \(b^{3/4}\). Measured rates approach it
  slowly from above.
- On the complete tic-tac-toe tree, alpha-beta proves the draw from 7 330 of
  255 168 final positions in square order and 781 with perfect ordering.
  Narrow windows answer yes/no questions more cheaply still.

```{cpp, eval="no"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
namespace alg = dm::algorithms;

auto tree = alg::tictactoe::game_tree();                   // or random_game_tree(d, b, seed)
auto r    = alg::alpha_beta(tree, /*maximizing=*/true);    // r.value, r.leaves_evaluated, r.cutoffs
auto best = alg::order_moves(tree, true, alg::MoveOrder::BestFirst);
auto km   = alg::minimal_tree_leaves(3, 10);               // 3^5 + 3^5 - 1 = 485
auto R3   = alg::alpha_beta_branching_factor(3);           // Pearl: 2.148
```
